<p class="eyebrow">FIELD JOURNAL / 01</p>
<h1>Archived Field Notes</h1>
<p class="intro">A saved webpage, printed as a document.</p>
<img src="https://example.invalid/diagram.svg" width="240" height="96" alt="A small diagram of three connected stages">
<h2>What is inside this archive?</h2>
<p>This MHTML contains the HTML document, a stylesheet, and an image in a single file. The original website does not need to exist for these resources to appear.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Saved with the page</th></tr></thead>
<tbody><tr><td>Text and structure</td><td>HTML part</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typography and page layout</td><td>CSS part</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diagram</td><td>Image part</td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p>Text remains selectable in the resulting PDF. The browser's print layout handles page size, margins, and normal content flow.</p>
<p class="screen-only">This screen-only sentence should not appear in the PDF.</p>
<section class="next-page">
<p class="eyebrow">FIELD JOURNAL / 02</p>
<h1>The Next Page</h1>
<p>This section begins on page two because the archived stylesheet contains an explicit page break.</p>
<h2>Longer documents</h2>
<p>Long paragraphs and lists flow onto additional pages automatically. You do not need to split the archive into separate files.</p>
<p>An archive can only contain what was saved. Missing images, unloaded content, and a site's restrictive print styles cannot always be repaired during conversion.</p>
<p class="ending">End of the archived notes.</p>
</section>
</body>
</html>

